# The checks result is REGENERATED on merge, never merged.
#
# `.claude/productizer/checks-result.json` records one run of the checks stage:
# which files it examined, what each check found, what the verdict was. Two
# branches that both ran the suite differ in it, so every merge of the two
# conflicts — it happened on a cherry-pick and on all three rebases of a
# three-PR stack in a single day.
#
# Every ordinary resolution of that conflict is a fabrication. Taking one side
# keeps a `change.files` naming that side's files while the tree now carries
# both; hand-editing the markers assembles a run nobody performed. Either way
# the file ends up describing a measurement that was never taken, which is P1
# — "a value that was not measured is never recorded as a measurement" — with
# a merge tool holding the pen.
#
# So this path is handed to a driver that resolves it the only honest way, by
# measuring the merged tree, and refuses whenever it cannot.
#
# THIS LINE ON ITS OWN DOES NOTHING. A merge driver has to be DEFINED in local
# config, and `.gitattributes` cannot define one — that is git's design, not an
# oversight: a repository that could name a program to run would be choosing
# what executes on the machine that cloned it, which is P4. So a fresh clone
# has no driver and this line is inert. Git says nothing about it and the merge
# takes the ordinary conflict, which is the safe failure. Activate it per
# clone, and read the header of the driver first — the second line grants it
# permission to run the merged tree's own check scripts:
#
#   git config merge.productizer-checks-result.driver \
#     "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/plugins/productizer/skills/spec/scripts/merge-checks-result.sh %O %A %B %L %P %X %Y"
#   git config merge.productizer-checks-result.trustrepo true
#
# Check what git can actually see, rather than assuming it is on:
#
#   plugins/productizer/skills/spec/scripts/merge-checks-result.sh --status
#
.claude/productizer/checks-result.json merge=productizer-checks-result
